a general-purpose workstation that needs to do demanding things while also protecting a large amount of valuable personal data:

VMs, sometimes with heavy games
Wine
local LLMs
Git/GitHub
email
a large local photo/archive store
RAID
potentially hostile downloads/USB devices
privacy against surveillance
recovery from corruption/ransomware
and you want very little software and code

That means I would not make DarkArtix itself one giant fortress.

I'd make the host boring and trusted, and make everything interesting happen in disposable or compartmentalized environments.

The architecture I'd settle on
                         DARKARTIX HOST
                              │
                ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
                │             │             │
             TRUSTED       DATA VAULT     ISOLATED
             DOMAIN                       DOMAINS
                │             │             │
              dwm          encrypted       │
             shell         storage          │
             editor            │            │
                │             │       ┌─────┼─────┐
                │             │       │     │     │
                │             │    Browser Wine  USB
                │             │       │     │     │
                │             │       └─────┼─────┘
                │             │             │
                │             │          NETBOX
                │             │             │
                │             │          Internet
                │
                └─────── no automatic access ───────┘

The data vault is the important new piece.

1. Your files should be more protected than your applications

Your biggest asset isn't the browser.

It's probably the accumulated data:

photos
emails
documents
Git repositories
LLM models
VM images
personal archives

So I would make a rule:

The host and applications do not automatically have access to the data vault.

Instead:

DATA VAULT
    │
    ├── photos
    ├── email archive
    ├── documents
    ├── source code
    └── other personal data

is mounted/available only when you deliberately need it.

Your browser should never see it.

Your Wine applications should never see it.

A random downloaded binary should never see it.

A USB inspector should never see it.

That is much more powerful than trying to sandbox every program perfectly.

2. RAID is not your safety net

This is extremely important given what you said about "stable OS from brick."

RAID protects primarily against certain hardware failures.

It does not protect you from:

rm -rf
ransomware
malware
filesystem corruption
bad administrator action
stolen machine
fire

If ransomware can write to every RAID member, you've got a very healthy RAID containing encrypted garbage.

So I'd separate:

LIVE DATA
    ↓
RAID

from:

RECOVERY
    ↓
offline / disconnected backup

The minimalist solution doesn't need a giant backup system.

Something as conceptually simple as:

             DATA
              │
          ┌───┴───┐
          │       │
        RAID    BACKUP
                  │
              disconnected

is vastly more important than another 500 lines of sandbox code.

And periodically test restoring from it.

3. I'd actually keep the data vault on a separate filesystem

Something like:

/
├── system
├── home
│
└── vault
     ├── photos
     ├── mail
     ├── git
     └── documents

with the vault encrypted at rest.

Then the important question becomes:

Who gets the vault key?

Not:

"Did Firefox have permission to /home/user/Documents?"

If Firefox never receives the vault key and the vault isn't mounted, there is nothing for Firefox to access.

That's an enormously cleaner security model.

4. Your browser becomes disposable

This is where DarkBox makes sense.

darkbox firefox

gets:

bwrap
user namespace
PID namespace
IPC namespace
mount namespace
Landlock
restricted /dev
disposable home
no D-Bus
no vault
isolated network
isolated display

Landlock is particularly attractive here because it is an unprivileged kernel security mechanism designed specifically to add restrictions to existing permissions, and its rules can only further restrict a process rather than grant it new access.

That fits your "least code, defense in depth" philosophy extremely well.

5. Your browser should never directly touch the physical network

I agree with your previous idea completely.

Firefox
   │
   ▼
DarkBox
   │
   ▼
NETBOX
   │
   ▼
VPN / Tor / proxy / direct
   │
   ▼
physical NIC

The browser knows nothing about:

wlan0
eth0
your LAN
your router
your host routing table

It gets a virtual network.

If you destroy NetBox:

Firefox ──X── Internet

while:

HOST ───────── Internet

can remain unaffected.

That's an excellent containment property.

6. Don't put your privacy logic inside applications

This is another place I'd simplify.

Don't make every browser/application understand:

VPN
Tor
proxychains
DNS
kill switch
routing

Make the network domain understand those things.

For example:

                    NETBOX
                      │
               ┌──────┴──────┐
               │             │
             VPN            Tor
               │             │
               └──────┬──────┘
                      │
                   Internet

Then:

browser → NETBOX

That's all the browser needs to know.

It also means you can kill/rebuild the network environment without modifying applications.

7. Git deserves its own small compartment

Your GitHub credentials are valuable.

I would not put your SSH private key in the general host environment if you're serious about this architecture.

Instead:

Git work environment
    │
    ├── source tree
    ├── git
    ├── SSH/GitHub credential
    └── network

Your browser never gets that credential.

Your Wine applications never get that credential.

Your USB environment never gets that credential.

The credential belongs to the Git compartment.

And when you're finished, you can shut that environment down.

That's compartmentalization without requiring a VM for every activity.

8. Local LLMs are an interesting case

You have two different things:

LLM MODEL FILES
        +
LLM EXECUTION

Model files can be huge and valuable, so they belong in your data vault.

But executing an arbitrary model/application isn't automatically trustworthy.

I'd do:

vault/models/
       │
       │ read-only
       ▼
LLM environment
       │
       ├── no write access to vault
       ├── no credentials
       └── controlled network

So the model can consume your 80 GB model file without being able to modify the master copy.

That's a recurring principle:

Share large data read-only whenever possible.

9. Wine deserves the same treatment as a hostile application

Wine is particularly interesting because Windows applications are not something I'd put in the trusted host environment by default.

Give Wine:

Wine environment
├── disposable Windows prefix
├── disposable home
├── no vault
├── no SSH credentials
├── no D-Bus
├── controlled devices
└── NETBOX

If some random .exe turns out to be malicious:

destroy Wine environment

instead of spending an afternoon figuring out what it modified.

10. Heavy games / VMs are the awkward exception

This is where I would not force everything through bwrap.

You sometimes need:

GPU
large memory
high I/O
VMs
gaming
Wine

Trying to make those behave like tiny desktop sandboxes could make DarkArtix miserable.

Instead, use a VM when you actually need VM isolation.

For example:

HOST
 │
 ├── normal Linux
 │
 └── gaming VM
       ├── GPU
       ├── game
       └── disposable/controlled environment

And importantly:

Don't give the gaming VM your data vault.

Gaming performance and security don't need to be enemies if the valuable data isn't exposed to the guest.

11. USB should be its own workflow

I'd keep the model we developed:

USB insertion
      ↓
DO NOT AUTOMOUNT
      ↓
user: "inspect"
      ↓
USBBOX
      ↓
filesystem mounted there
      ↓
inspect
      ↓
explicitly export selected files
      ↓
destroy USBBOX

The trusted host receives copies, not a mounted USB filesystem.

And USBBox gets:

network = NONE
vault = NONE
credentials = NONE
host home = NONE

That's an extremely clean boundary.

There is a deeper limitation: if the host kernel itself handles the physical USB controller/device, this is not equivalent to Qubes' dedicated USB qube. Qubes isolates USB controllers specifically to reduce exposure of its trusted domain to malicious USB devices.

So I would call your first version USB filesystem isolation, not full USB-controller isolation.

12. Your "surveillance" goal needs a different mental model

This is important.

There are at least three different threats:

Network surveillance
ISP
Wi-Fi observer
LAN attacker
website tracking
DNS observation

NetBox + VPN/Tor can help here.

Application compromise
malicious website
malicious file
malicious executable

DarkBox helps here.

Host compromise
kernel exploit
firmware compromise
malicious hypervisor
physical attacker

None of the above guarantees protection here.

And no Linux design can honestly promise "surveillance-proof."

For example, if you log into Google, Google knows that account activity. Tor doesn't change that fundamental fact.

Privacy architecture needs to distinguish:

hiding network metadata

from:

hiding identity

from:

preventing endpoint compromise.

They're different problems.

13. I'd prioritize recovery above almost everything

You said something very revealing:

"all my files safe ... meaning more this time as stable os from brick"

Then recovery should be one of DarkArtix's first-class security features, not an afterthought.

Imagine:

DarkArtix gets completely destroyed.

Your ideal response should be:

reinstall DarkArtix
        ↓
restore configuration
        ↓
unlock data vault
        ↓
restore personal data
        ↓
continue

Not:

"Oh shit, my entire digital life was on that machine."

Your OS should therefore be replaceable.

Your data should not be.

That's a very powerful minimalist philosophy:

The operating system is disposable. The data is not.

14. This actually makes DarkArtix simpler

Notice what happens when we design it this way.

We don't need DarkArtix to protect everything equally.

We have:

                    DARKARTIX
                        │
       ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
       │                │                │
     HOST             VAULT            BOXES
       │                │                │
    minimal          encrypted        disposable
    trusted          important        untrusted

The host doesn't need to be a fortress containing every feature.

It needs to be small enough that you can understand it.

The vault doesn't need to run applications.

It needs to store things safely.

The boxes don't need to persist.

They need to contain things you don't trust.

That's an extremely clean division.

15. My final design for your use case

I'd aim for this:

                         DARKARTIX
                             │
                    ┌────────┴────────┐
                    │                 │
                 TRUSTED           UNTRUSTED
                    │                 │
                  dwm              DARKBOX
                    │                 │
              ┌─────┼─────┐      ┌────┼────┐
              │     │     │      │    │    │
            shell  git   admin  web  wine USB
              │           │      │    │    │
              │           │      └────┼────┘
              │           │           │
              │           │         NETBOX
              │           │           │
              │           │       VPN/Tor/etc
              │           │
              └───────────┼───────────────┐
                          │               │
                       DATA VAULT      OFFLINE
                          │             BACKUP
                          │
                     RAID/storage

And I'd establish these rules:

Rule 1: The host is replaceable.

Rule 2: The vault is not automatically mounted.

Rule 3: Untrusted applications never see the vault.

Rule 4: Untrusted applications don't see physical networking.

Rule 5: USB is never automatically trusted or mounted.

Rule 6: Promotion means explicit copying, never mounting the untrusted environment into the host.

Rule 7: Disposable environments disappear after use.

Rule 8: Git credentials live only where Git needs them.

Rule 9: Large model/data stores are exposed read-only whenever possible.

Rule 10: RAID is availability; separate offline backups are recovery.

Rule 11: Heavy workloads get VMs when appropriate instead of contorting the lightweight sandbox.

Rule 12: No permanent DarkBox daemon.

And I would not add much more.

I wouldn't add SELinux just because "more security is better."

I wouldn't add AppArmor just because it sounds useful.

I wouldn't add a security daemon.

I wouldn't add a policy database.

I wouldn't build a custom encrypted filesystem.

I wouldn't implement my own VPN.

I wouldn't write my own Tor implementation.

I wouldn't build a custom VM manager.

And I wouldn't make DarkArtix automatically sandbox everything.

Use the kernel primitives that already exist.

Namespaces + bwrap + Landlock + nftables + LUKS + VMs where actually useful + disciplined backups.

Landlock is particularly attractive because its design is explicitly to let unprivileged applications add restrictions to themselves, rather than requiring another privileged policy daemon.

The result isn't "the most secure Linux possible."

It's something I think is much more valuable for your goal:

A small Linux system where the things most likely to be compromised are disposable, the things most valuable are normally inaccessible, the network is a replaceable compartment, removable media is treated as hostile until proven otherwise, and the entire operating system can be thrown away and rebuilt without losing your life.

That's the security/minimalism balance I'd optimize DarkArtix around.
